# QR Ordering MVP — Project Rules

Full architecture, schema, UX, and business context lives in `/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md` at the project root. Read it before generating any backend, schema, or page-level code if you haven't already loaded it this session.

## Stack — do not deviate without being explicitly asked
- Next.js App Router + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS only. No CSS-in-JS, no separate CSS modules unless explicitly requested.
- Supabase for auth, database, and realtime. No custom Node/Express backend, no separate API server.
- Use the Supabase JS client directly from Server Components/Server Actions where possible. Only add a Next.js API route when logic must run server-side and can't live in Postgres/RLS (e.g. computing order totals server-side so a client can't tamper with prices).

## Schema conventions
- Every table (except pure lookup tables) has a `restaurant_id` foreign key — this is multi-tenant from day one, never omit it.
- Money fields are `numeric(10,2)`, never `float`.
- `order_items.price_at_order_time` is a snapshot taken at insert time — never join live `menu_items.price` to display historical orders.
- `tables.qr_token` is a random UUID, never a sequential/guessable value.

## Build discipline
- Build only what's explicitly asked for in the current prompt. Do not add features, pages, or "nice to haves" that weren't requested, even if related.
- Don't scaffold a whole feature area in one shot — one screen or one flow per prompt, following the step order in PROJECT_CONTEXT.md section 2.
- Don't install a new dependency without stating which one and why first.
- Don't modify the database schema (directly or via MCP) once real restaurant data exists in a table without flagging it first and confirming.

## Explicitly out of scope for MVP — do not build unless asked
- Payment gateway integration (eSewa/Khalti/FonePay)
- Kitchen Display System
- Inventory management
- Analytics/reporting dashboards
- Native mobile app
- Multi-branch/franchise management
- Full i18n framework — hardcode two string sets (English/Nepali) instead

## Auth
- Customers: no login, ever. Identity comes from the `qr_token` in the URL only.
- Admin/staff: Supabase Auth email/password, scoped to `restaurant_id` via RLS — not app-level checks alone.
